# mm.fs

A portable file interface: one API for every volume, whatever holds the files
-- FAT on a card, littlefs in flash, a directory of the host's file system.
It holds no driver and needs no provider; drivers implement Volume, and
devices implement BlockDevice or FlashDevice.

A path is absolute, '/'-separated UTF-8, and normalize makes it canonical
before any driver sees it, so a driver can never be handed a ".." to escape
with. A bounded table maps a mount prefix -- "/" or "/" followed by one
component -- to a Volume the caller owns, and every path call resolves to the
longest mounted prefix.

File and Directory are move-only values that close on destruction. mm.fs
counts what is open on each mount, refuses to unmount a volume in use, and
checks a File's access itself, so a read of a file opened for writing is
BadArgument on every volume alike. What a driver must promise beyond that is
written on Volume and checked by mm.fs.conformance.

The clock is a function pointer a program installs, so mm.fs names no clock
module. McuStorage is mm.mcu's block storage as a BlockDevice, the only
device mm.fs supplies itself; it is why the module uses mm.mcu.

Nothing here allocates. mm.fs serves one caller at a time: its path buffers
and mount table are static.
